# Task 6 – Design the Inheritance Hierarchy

## Objective
To design an inheritance hierarchy for vehicles using Java.

## 1. Inheritance Hierarchy

Vehicle is the superclass.

- Car extends Vehicle.
- TwoWheeler extends Vehicle.
- ElectricCar extends Car.

This design uses hierarchical and multilevel inheritance.

## 2. Superclass and Subclasses

Vehicle:
Stores common vehicle information such as registration number.

Car:
Inherits the registration number from Vehicle.

TwoWheeler:
Represents two-wheeler vehicles and inherits Vehicle properties.

ElectricCar:
Inherits properties from Car and represents an electric car.

## 3. Use of super Keyword

The super keyword calls the constructor of the parent class.
It initializes the registration number and avoids repeating code.

## 4. Constructor Execution Order

When an ElectricCar object is created, constructors execute in this order:

1. Vehicle constructor
2. Car constructor
3. ElectricCar constructor

The parent constructor executes before the child constructor.

## 5. UML Class Diagram

             Vehicle
             /     \
           Car   TwoWheeler
            |
        ElectricCar

## 6. Alternative Concept: Composition

Instead of inheritance, a Vehicle class could contain a VehicleType
object that stores the vehicle category and hourly rate.

Inheritance represents an IS-A relationship.
Composition represents a HAS-A relationship.

## 7. Complexity Analysis

Inheritance:
- Fee calculation: O(1)
- Additional relationship storage: O(1) per vehicle, excluding object overhead.

Composition:
- Fee calculation: O(1)
- Stores a VehicleType reference per vehicle.

Adding a new vehicle type using inheritance generally requires
creating a new subclass. With composition, a new type can often
be added by creating a new VehicleType object without creating
another Vehicle subclass.

## Conclusion
Inheritance reduces code duplication and supports code reuse.
The hierarchy can be extended by adding new vehicle subclasses.